**Answer: Yes.**

**Proof.** We show that under the given hypotheses, $\|f_n - f\|_p \to 0$.

Since $f_n \to f$ a.e., we have $|f_n - f|^p \to 0$ a.e. as well. The key tool is Fatou's lemma applied to a carefully chosen non-negative sequence.

For $p \geq 1$, the convexity of $t \mapsto |t|^p$ gives the pointwise inequality
$$|f_n - f|^p \leq 2^{p-1}\bigl(|f_n|^p + |f|^p\bigr).$$
Define
$$\Phi_n := 2^{p-1}\bigl(|f_n|^p + |f|^p\bigr) - |f_n - f|^p \;\geq\; 0.$$

Since $f_n \to f$ a.e., we have $|f_n|^p \to |f|^p$ a.e. and $|f_n - f|^p \to 0$ a.e., so
$$\lim_{n\to\infty} \Phi_n = 2^{p-1}\bigl(|f|^p + |f|^p\bigr) - 0 = 2^p\,|f|^p \quad\text{a.e.}$$

Applying Fatou's lemma to the non-negative functions $\Phi_n$:
$$\int_\Omega 2^p|f|^p \;=\; \int_\Omega \liminf_{n\to\infty} \Phi_n \;\leq\; \liminf_{n\to\infty} \int_\Omega \Phi_n.$$

Computing the right-hand side:
$$\int_\Omega \Phi_n = 2^{p-1}\bigl(\|f_n\|_p^p + \|f\|_p^p\bigr) - \|f_n - f\|_p^p.$$

Since $\|f_n\|_p \to \|f\|_p$, we have $\|f_n\|_p^p \to \|f\|_p^p$, so
$$\liminf_{n\to\infty} \int_\Omega \Phi_n = 2^{p-1}\bigl(\|f\|_p^p + \|f\|_p^p\bigr) - \limsup_{n\to\infty}\|f_n - f\|_p^p = 2^p\|f\|_p^p - \limsup_{n\to\infty}\|f_n - f\|_p^p.$$

Combining the Fatou inequality:
$$2^p\|f\|_p^p \;\leq\; 2^p\|f\|_p^p - \limsup_{n\to\infty}\|f_n - f\|_p^p.$$

Since $\|f\|_p < \infty$ (as $f \in L^p$), we conclude
$$\limsup_{n\to\infty}\|f_n - f\|_p^p \;\leq\; 0.$$

As $\|f_n - f\|_p^p \geq 0$, this forces $\|f_n - f\|_p^p \to 0$, and hence
$$\|f_n - f\|_p \to 0. \qquad\blacksquare$$

